  "live_records": {
    "q-48459": {
      "dom_id": "step-q48459",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14193250/assets/72e1d1f1ea1aa635441da5503a49ac4d33d5ab0df62d48aa699debc7816df1c2.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the graph of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above? \n\nI. ${lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-2+h)-f(-2)}{h}=1$\n\nII. ${lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(h)-f(0)}{h}=-0.5$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-3+h)-f(-3)}{h}=1$",
      "worked_solution": "Comparing these limits with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h},\n$$\n\nwe see that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-2+h)-f(-2)}{h} & ={f}^{′}(-2), \\\\ {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(h)-f(0)}{h} & ={f}^{′}(0), \\\\ {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-3+h)-f(-3)}{h} & ={f}^{′}(-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function consists of three line segments. Inspecting the graph, we see that\n\n- for $x<-1$, we have  ${f}^{′}(x)=1,$\n\n- for $-1<x<1$, we have  ${f}^{′}(x)=-1,$\n\n- for $x>1$, we have  ${f}^{′}(x)=1.$\n\nThe derivative does not exist at $x=-1$ and $x=1$ . So we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-2+h)-f(-2)}{h} & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(h)-f(0)}{h} & =-1, \\\\ {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-3+h)-f(-3)}{h} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only I and III are true.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
