    "q-104948": {
      "dom_id": "step-q104948",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{x-2}=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we let\n\n$$\nL={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{x-2}.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a},\n$$\n\nwe see that if we let $f(x)={x}^{2}$ and $a=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{f(x)-f(2)}{x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{x-2}=L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis confirms that $L={f}^{′}(2).$\n\nComputing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2x,\\,{f}^{′}(2)=2\\cdot 2=4,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $L=4.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
