    "q-339039": {
      "dom_id": "step-q339039",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{{x}^{2}-25}{x-5}=${{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we let\n\n$$\nL={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{{x}^{2}-25}{x-5}.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a},\n$$\n\nwe see that if we let $f(x)={x}^{2}$ and $a=5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(5) & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{f(x)-f(5)}{x-5} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{5}^{2}}{x-5} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{{x}^{2}-25}{x-5}=L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis confirms that $L={f}^{′}(5).$\n\nComputing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2x,\\,{f}^{′}(5)=2\\cdot 5=10,\n$$\n\nand therefore, we conclude that $L=10.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
